-- Measured behaviour (tests/rls.sql, 2026-08-31)
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
--
--                        trainer t-001   client c-001   client c-002   anon
--   client_profiles              8              1              -          0
--   workout_logs               144             44             10          0
--   logged_sets               2290            700              -          0
--   nutrition_days             177             45              -          0
--   body_metrics                 -             72              -          -
--   threads                      8              1              1          0
--   messages                    27              6              -          0
--   users                        9              2              -          -
--   exercises                   32             32              -          0
--
--   c-001 rows visible to c-002: 0.  Other clients' logs visible to c-001: 0.
--
-- Re-run 2026-09-19 after the two edits above (offline Postgres 16, whole
-- migration chain + seed.sql): every number in the table is unchanged, and the
-- self-signup cases read --
--
--   an individual updating their own macros        1 row   (client_profiles_self_writes)
--   a coached client updating their own macros     0 rows  (unchanged; complete_intake only)
--   an individual updating another client's row    0 rows
--   an individual creating their own routine       ok, trainer_id null, author_id theirs
--   a coached client self-planning a routine       ok      (unchanged)
--
-- 2026-09-19, role-escalation edits (users_self_update dropped, app_role()
-- added to trainer_profiles_self and client_profiles_trainer_insert): NOT
-- replayed -- no test was asked for. tests/rls.sql carries the three cases
-- (c1_role_after_self_promote, c1_mints_trainer_profile, c1_fake_roster) and
-- they run with the rest of this file at T10.
--
-- 2026-09-20, billing (20260920000001). Re-run offline against the whole chain
-- + seed.sql + this file. plan_active() went inside owns_client(), so the gate
-- is the predicate ~20 tables already route through, not a clause per policy.
--
--                                   t-001 paid   t-001 lapsed   c-001
--   client_profiles                        8            0          1
--   workout_logs                         144            0         44
--   logged_sets                         2290            0          -
--   body_metrics                         504            0          -
--   nutrition_days                         -            0          -
--   threads                                -            -          1
--
--   Every paid-coach number above is the one recorded in the table further up:
--   a coach with a plan sees exactly what they saw before this existed.
--
--   a lapsed coach updating a client's macros      0 rows
--   a client reading their coach's billing         0 rows (they have none)
--   a client writing subscriptions                 permission denied (grant, not policy)
--   an individual reading their own subscription   1 row, solo/trialing, 14 days
--   an individual editing their own macros         1 row (unchanged by billing)
--   anon                                           0 everywhere (unchanged)
--
-- 2026-09-20, second run: `tests/rls.sql` now runs to completion for the first
-- time since the S4 edits -- 66 cases, every number above reproduced exactly.
-- It had been failing on a line that predates all of this: the seed writes
-- explicit ids without advancing id_sequences, so next_id('c') minted c-001 and
-- invite_client collided on users_pkey. Fixed at the source (seed.sql and
-- seed.mjs now end with a derived counter bump), not here.
--
--   restored_clients reads 9, not 8, and that is correct: the onboarding
--   section above invites two clients and revokes one before the billing cases
--   run. It is the count after that sequence, not a discrepancy.
